<header class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Case study · Automation</div>
  <h1>From re-typing reports to one drag-and-drop</h1>
  <p class="lead">A private, browser-based tool that reads motor valuation reports and builds the monthly Excel schedule automatically. No uploads, no storage, and every figure checked before it reaches the spreadsheet.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span><b>Built by</b> Abraham Otieno</span>
    <span><b>For</b> an independent vehicle valuation firm (client kept confidential)</span>
    <span><b>Type</b> Workflow automation · Data extraction · Web tool</span>
  </div>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap">

<section>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>Every valuation report is a multi-page PDF. To bill insurers, someone had to open each one and copy five details into a monthly Excel schedule: inspection date, insurer, registration, policy number and report reference. Then work out the valuation type and the fee, and check that no report had been entered twice.</p>
  <p>It was slow, easy to get wrong, and the documents are highly confidential, so sending them to an online service was not an option.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The solution</h2>
  <p>Drop a report, a whole folder, or a scan into the page. The tool reads it, fills in the row, works out the fee, warns about anything doubtful, and produces a ready-to-use Excel file that matches the firm's existing template. Nothing else from the report leaves the page.</p>
  <figure>
    <div class="shot"><img src="assets/screenshot-batch.jpg" alt="The tool showing four sample reports: two ready, two flagged as repeats, with the report page and highlighted source values beside the editable row"></div>
    <figcaption>Sample reports (fictional data). Clicking a field highlights exactly where on the report that value was read from.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div><b>Drop in reports</b><span>PDFs, scanned PDFs, photos or entire folders. Digital PDFs are read directly; scans go through OCR.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Each value is found by its label</b><span>The reader locates a label such as "Policy No" and takes the text beside it, including wrapped lines, so a value always belongs to its own label, whatever order the PDF stores its text in.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Type and fee come from the report heading</b><span>"Valuation Report" is Standard (1,000). "Digital Report" or "AI Report" is Digital/AI (900). Nothing to select by hand.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Checks run automatically</b><span>Cross-checks, repeat detection and OCR safeguards (below) decide whether a row is Ready or needs a human look.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Download the Excel schedule</b><span>Only clean rows are exported, in the firm's own column layout with the Month and lookup formulas intact.</span></div></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Built for accuracy</h2>
  <div class="grid g3">
    <div class="card"><h3>Cross-checks</h3><p>The inspection date is compared with the date embedded in the report reference and with photo timestamps. The registration is compared with the file name. Any mismatch is flagged.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Repeat detection</h3><p>Catches the same file, the same report reference, or the same vehicle, policy and date, within a batch and against an existing schedule the user attaches.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Human in the loop for scans</h3><p>OCR can misread characters, so every scanned field is flagged and must be confirmed against the page image before it is exported.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Confidentiality by design</h2>
  <div class="note">The reports never leave the user's computer.</div>
  <div class="grid g2 u-margin-top-16px">
    <div class="card"><h3>Runs entirely in the browser</h3><p>There is no server, database or upload. The PDF reader, OCR engine and Excel writer are bundled with the tool and run locally.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Locked to its own origin</h3><p>A strict Content-Security-Policy stops the page sending data anywhere else. It was tested with zero blocked or attempted outside requests.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Nothing is stored</h3><p>No cookies, local storage or caches. Closing the tab erases everything, and it wipes itself after 30 minutes of inactivity.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>One output</h3><p>The only thing that leaves is the Excel file the user chooses to download, containing just the schedule columns.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Results</h2>
  <div class="grid g3">
    <div class="card"><div class="stat">~20 ms<small>to read one digital report</small></div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="stat">~9 s<small>for 100 reports of about 5 MB each</small></div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="stat">2–4 s<small>per scanned page, several read in parallel</small></div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="u-margin-top-16px u-color-var-mute u-font-size-15px">Measured in a browser test on a laptop. Reading a batch does not freeze the page; page previews load only when a file is opened.</p>
</section>

